messages.text:
The diff-classification pass has started, covering the 349 promotions merged between Oct 22 and Nov 1. The helper is working from current Quicksilver `origin/master` and parses each master diff with a Rust syntax parser. It sorts every change into one of these:
- function-body-only (the narrow case);
- structural edits by crate (the crate-level fallback, which in practice means rerunning the root suites);
- fixture and data changes;
- build and manifest changes (full rerun);
- docs and agent files (no effect).

It will report what share of promotions the function-level rule would actually have narrowed, and which kinds of structural edits are most common. I'll summarize when it's done.

The first map attempt stopped on a red baseline. 15 of 22,050 tests failed on the VM, mostly from environment differences such as Go 1.22 against CI's 1.26, plus goldens that may already be red on master. I've excluded those 15 with Quicksilver's `[map] skip-tests` and restarted. It's rerunning the 37-minute baseline, then the instrumented build and run. The VM self-terminates at 14:05 UTC, which leaves room.

When `sitemap.json` exists, I'll pull it back and match each promotion's body-edited functions to the map's functions, by file path and name, since line numbers drift between trees. Then I'll count how many tests entered any of them, which is the lower bound.

Waiting on you: whether to implement the triage benchmark fixes:
1. no re-review of regressions an open ticket already tracks;
2. announce only new or reopened tickets;
3. one benchmark investigation per morning.
